const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"notes/Omics/genomics/bwa/bwa-en","title":"BWA MEM Alignment to Human Genome in Practice","tags":["BWA","Genome Alignment","NGS","Command Line","Human Genome","SAM/BAM"],"path":"notes/Omics/genomics/bwa/bwa-en","content":"BWA MEM Alignment to Human Genome in Practice Basic Commands # Index the reference genome bwa index ref.fa # Align paired-end sequencing data bwa mem -t 8 ref.fa read1.fq read2.fq > output.sam # Convert to BAM format and sort samtools view -bS output.sam | samtools sort -o output.bam Parameter Optimization -t : Number of threads, adjust based on server configuration -M : Mark shorter split hits as secondary -R : Add read group information"},{"id":"notes/Omics/proteomics/maxquant/maxquant-en","title":"MaxQuant Proteomics Analysis Workflow","tags":["MaxQuant","Proteomics","Mass Spectrometry","Bioinformatics","Quantitative Analysis"],"path":"notes/Omics/proteomics/maxquant/maxquant-en","content":"MaxQuant Proteomics Analysis Workflow Basic Configuration Input File Settings RAW files: Mass spectrometry raw data FASTA files: Protein database Main Parameters Enzyme: Trypsin/P Maximum missed cleavages: 2 Precursor mass tolerance: 20 ppm (MS1) Fragment mass tolerance: 0.5 Da (MS2) Analysis Workflow # Run MaxQuant mono MaxQuantCmd.exe mqpar.xml Typical output files: evidence.txt: Peptide identification results proteinGroups.txt: Protein quantification results summary.txt: Analysis statistics summary"},{"id":"notes/Omics/transcriptomics/rnaseq-hisat2/rnaseq-hisat2-en","title":"RNA-Seq Analysis: HISAT2 Alignment Workflow","tags":["RNA-Seq","HISAT2","Transcriptome","Alignment","Bioinformatics"],"path":"notes/Omics/transcriptomics/rnaseq-hisat2/rnaseq-hisat2-en","content":"RNA-Seq Analysis: HISAT2 Alignment Workflow HISAT2 Features Supports splice-aware alignment Efficient index structure Suitable for RNA-Seq reads of various lengths Basic Commands # Build index hisat2-build ref.fa ref_index # Align RNA-Seq data hisat2 -x ref_index -1 read1.fq -2 read2.fq -S output.sam"},{"id":"notes/Programming/python/biopython/biopython-en","title":"Biopython Bioinformatics Applications","tags":["Python","Biopython","Bioinformatics","Sequence Analysis","Genomics"],"path":"notes/Programming/python/biopython/biopython-en","content":"Biopython Bioinformatics Applications Sequence Operations from Bio import SeqIO from Bio.Seq import Seq # Read FASTA file for record in SeqIO.parse( \\"sequences.fasta\\" , \\"fasta\\" ): print (record. id , len (record.seq)) # Sequence translation dna_seq = Seq( \\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\\" ) protein_seq = dna_seq.translate()"},{"id":"notes/Programming/python/fastq/fastq-en","title":"Processing FASTQ Quality Statistics with Pandas","tags":["Python","Pandas","FASTQ","Data Cleaning","Bioinformatics","Beginner"],"path":"notes/Programming/python/fastq/fastq-en","content":"Processing FASTQ Quality Statistics with Pandas Brief example: Reading fastqc_data.txt and extracting basic statistics: import pandas as pd df = pd.read_csv( \\"fastqc_data.txt\\" , sep= \\"\\\\t\\" , comment= \\"#\\" ) print (df.head())"},{"id":"notes/Programming/python/pandas/pandas-en","title":"Python Pandas Data Processing in Practice","tags":["Python","Pandas","Data Processing","Data Analysis","Data Cleaning"],"path":"notes/Programming/python/pandas/pandas-en","content":"Python Pandas Data Processing in Practice Data Reading import pandas as pd # Read CSV file df = pd.read_csv( 'data.csv' ) # Read Excel file df = pd.read_excel( 'data.xlsx' ) Data Cleaning # Handle missing values df.fillna( 0 , inplace= True ) # Remove duplicate rows df.drop_duplicates(inplace= True )"},{"id":"notes/Programming/r/demo/demo-en","title":"R Language Data Processing Example","tags":["R Language","Data Processing","Statistical Analysis","Data Visualization","Bioinformatics"],"path":"notes/Programming/r/demo/demo-en","content":"R Language Data Processing Example Data Reading and Basic Operations # Read CSV file data &#x3C;- read.csv ( \\"expression_data.csv\\" ) # View data structure str ( data ) head ( data ) # Basic statistics summary ( data ) Data Visualization # Install and load ggplot2 install.packages ( \\"ggplot2\\" ) library ( ggplot2 ) # Create scatter plot ggplot ( data , aes ( x = Condition , y = Expression ) ) + geom_boxplot ( ) + theme_minimal ( ) + labs ( title = \\"Gene Expression Level Comparison\\" ) Statistical Analysis # t-test t_test_result &#x3C;- t.test ( Expression ~ Condition , data = data ) print ( t_test_result )"},{"id":"notes/Programming/r/ggplot2/ggplot2-en","title":"R Language ggplot2 Data Visualization","tags":["R Language","ggplot2","Data Visualization","Statistical Charts","Bioinformatics"],"path":"notes/Programming/r/ggplot2/ggplot2-en","content":"R Language ggplot2 Data Visualization Basic Syntax library ( ggplot2 ) # Create scatter plot ggplot ( data , aes ( x = expression , y = condition ) ) + geom_point ( ) + theme_minimal ( ) + labs ( title = \\"Gene Expression Scatter Plot\\" ) Common Chart Types Box plot: geom_boxplot() Bar chart: geom_bar() Line chart: geom_line()"},{"id":"notes/Programming/shell/bash/bash-en","title":"Bash Shell Scripting","tags":["Shell","Bash","Command Line","Scripting","Automation"],"path":"notes/Programming/shell/bash/bash-en","content":"Bash Shell Scripting Basic Syntax #!/bin/bash # Variable definition input_file= \\"data.fastq\\" output_dir= \\"results\\" # Conditional judgment if [ -f \\" $input_file \\" ]; then echo \\"File exists\\" else echo \\"File does not exist\\" fi Loop Processing # Iterate through files for file in *.fastq; do echo \\"Processing file: $file \\" done"},{"id":"projects/Biocrawler/api/api-demo/api-demo-en","title":"BioCrawler API Documentation","tags":[],"path":"projects/Biocrawler/api/api-demo/api-demo-en","content":"BioCrawler API Documentation The following content is entirely fictional! API Overview Provides RESTful API interfaces for querying and downloading biological data. Core Interfaces 1. Sequence Query Interface GET /api/v1/sequence/{accession_id} Parameter Description: accession_id : Database accession number (e.g., NM_001301717) Response Example: { \\"accession\\" : \\"NM_001301717\\" , \\"sequence\\" : \\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\\" , \\"length\\" : 38 , \\"organism\\" : \\"Homo sapiens\\" } 2. Batch Download Interface POST /api/v1/batch Request Body: { \\"accessions\\" : [ \\"NM_001301717\\" , \\"NM_001301718\\" ] , \\"format\\" : \\"fasta\\" } Authentication Method # Using API Key curl -H \\"X-API-Key: your_key_here\\" \\\\ https://api.biocrawler.example.com/api/v1/sequence/NM_001301717 Error Handling Status Code Description 200 Success 404 Data Not Found 429 Rate Limit Exceeded 500 Server Error Usage Limits Free Tier: 100 requests per hour Pro Tier: 1000 requests per hour"},{"id":"projects/Biocrawler/installation/installation-demo/installation-demo-en","title":"BioCrawler Installation Guide","tags":[],"path":"projects/Biocrawler/installation/installation-demo/installation-demo-en","content":"BioCrawler Installation Guide The following content is entirely fictional! Environment Requirements Python 3.8+ pip 20.0+ Operating System: Windows 10+, macOS 10.15+, Linux Quick Installation 1. Install via pip pip install biocrawler 2. Install from Source git clone https://github.com/example/biocrawler.git cd biocrawler pip install -e . Configuration Settings Create Configuration File # Initialize configuration biocrawler init # Edit configuration file nano ~/.biocrawler/config.yaml Configuration Example api: key: \\"your_api_key_here\\" timeout: 30 database: cache_dir: \\"~/.biocrawler/cache\\" max_cache_size: 10GB download: threads: 4 retries: 3 Verify Installation # Test connection biocrawler test # Check version biocrawler --version # Show help biocrawler -- help Frequently Asked Questions Q: What should I do if the installation fails? A: Ensure Python version >= 3.8 and try updating pip Q: How to update? A: pip install --upgrade biocrawler Docker Installation docker pull biocrawler/biocrawler:latest docker run -it biocrawler/biocrawler:latest"},{"id":"topics/StructureOfProteinDemo/methods/methods-demo/methods-demo-en","title":"Experimental Methods","tags":[],"path":"topics/StructureOfProteinDemo/methods/methods-demo/methods-demo-en","content":"Experimental Methods The following content is entirely fictional! 1. Sample Preparation Protein Expression and Purification # Expression system Host: E. coli BL21(DE3) Induction conditions: 0.5 mM IPTG, 18°C, 16 hours # Purification steps 1. Ni-NTA affinity chromatography 2. Gel filtration chromatography (Superdex 200) 3. Concentrate to 3 mg/mL Negative Stain EM Screening Stain: 2% Uranyl acetate Data acquisition: 200 kV, 50,000x magnification Screening criteria: Monodisperse, intact particles 2. Cryo-EM Data Collection Sample Preparation Apply 3 μL sample onto Quantifoil R1.2/1.3 grid Blot with filter paper, rapid freezing in liquid nitrogen Working temperature: -180°C Acquisition Parameters Parameter Value Acceleration voltage 300 kV Pixel size 1.5 Å Electron dose 50 e⁻/Å² Number of frames 50 frames 3. Data Processing Motion Correction Software: MotionCor2 Corrects inter-frame motion and drift Particle Picking Method: Template matching Software: RELION 3.1 Number of particles: ~50,000 2D Classification Number of classes: 20 Selection criteria: Clear features 4. 3D Reconstruction Initial Model Method: Random conical tilt reconstruction Resolution: ~8 Å Refined Reconstruction Iterations: 20 rounds Resolution: 3.0 Å Resolution criterion: FSC 0.143 5. Model Building and Refinement Initial Model Building Software: Coot Method: Automated modeling + manual correction Structure Refinement Software: Phenix.refine Refinement items: Atomic coordinates B-factors Occupancies Quality Validation Ramachandran plot: 96.5% favored regions Geometry deviations: Bond length 0.01 Å, bond angle 1.2° MolProbity score: 1.8"},{"id":"topics/StructureOfProteinDemo/results/results-demo/results-demo-en","title":"Experimental Results","tags":[],"path":"topics/StructureOfProteinDemo/results/results-demo/results-demo-en","content":"Experimental Results The following content is entirely fictional! 1. Data Collection Statistics Data Quality Assessment Metric Result Total Images 2,500 micrographs Total Dose 50 e⁻/Å² Signal-to-Noise Ratio 15:1 Total Particles 52,347 Motion Correction Effectiveness Drift before correction: ~50 Å Drift after correction: &#x3C; 2 Å Inter-frame correlation: > 0.95 2. 2D Classification Results Representative 2D Class Averages Show clear secondary structure features Particles are evenly distributed with no significant aggregation Clean background with minimal contamination Classification Statistics Valid classes: 8/20 Retained particles: 38,245 Reasons for discarding: blurring, aggregation, contamination 3. 3D Reconstruction Results Resolution Assessment FSC 0.143: 3.0 Å FSC 0.5: 2.8 Å Resolution distribution: Isotropic Electron Density Map Quality Main chain density: Continuous and clear Side chain density: Mostly visible Loop region density: Partially blurred 4. Model Quality Structural Features Number of residues: 428 Ligands: ATP (1) Water molecules: 126 Metal ions: Mg²⁺ (2) Geometric Quality Bond length RMSD: 0.012 Å Bond angle RMSD: 1.3° Torsion angle outliers: 3.2% Ramachandran Plot Favored regions: 96.5% Allowed regions: 3.2% Outlier regions: 0.3% 5. Structure-Function Analysis Active Site ATP binding pocket: Residues 45-60 Metal ion binding sites: Residues 120-130, 200-210 Substrate channel: Residues 250-280 Structural Comparison Similarity to homologous protein: 78% Key functional regions: Highly conserved Conformational differences: Active site in open state 6. Conclusion This study successfully determined the three-dimensional structure of the target protein at a resolution of 3.0 Å, with good model quality. The structure reveals: ATP binding mode Metal ion coordination environment Potential catalytic mechanism This structure provides an important foundation for subsequent functional studies and drug design."}]`);export{e as default};
